-
-## Follow-up validation
-
-The later source-bound client-profile run of Miami acquired all 32 roots twice and retained 828 records, 137 tables, 10,892 cells and 439 image placeholders. Both traversals captured the exact publisher JavaScript that explains its rendering transformation and fragment routing. Comparing the independent rendered witnesses against that exact transformation produces zero source-comparison errors; original native text remains unchanged. Unknown or changed client bundles do not receive this treatment. This run still has two unexplained empty entries and 672 unresolved reference occurrences, so it is not publication-complete. Binary and normalized-output image audits both passed with zero separate image binaries.
-
-The current normalizer also retains source-row context for references. Only the explicit top-level `COMPREHENSIVE ORDINANCE LIST`, with its observed `Ord. No.` / `Action` columns and matching bibliographic labels, qualifies as an ordinance reference inventory. Required appendices and links in operative provisions remain unresolved. Source rows, labels, URLs, ownership and hashes stay in the output. This classification does not claim collection of uncodified ordinances.
-
-The complete retained-source context replay preserved all 2,687 Cupertino records and all 2,289 ordinance-index references, plus two search controls. Seventeen other reference occurrences remain unresolved. Both full Miami and Cupertino derivatives had stable opening/closing inventories and passed image audits. These are offline derivatives of the bound live captures, not new downloads.
-
-Independent raw-browser QA subsequently found a defect that the collector's own paired normalizations had missed: one native Miami table cell joined `Transit Corridors:` to `NW 7th Avenue` at a nested list boundary. The active parser now inserts both block-start and block-end boundaries while preserving inline words, with a complete actual-root regression fixture. Older retained outputs preserve the failed text; a fresh derivative and independent audit are required before crediting that correction to a full publication. Custom list-marker classes and nonnumeric list styles are also under review; unresolved marker fidelity must not be silently counted as verified text.
-
-The newly discovered Miami Comprehensive Neighborhood Plan configuration is a separate publication. Its initial live run stopped at an unlabeled publisher root. A subsequent ordinary-browser diagnostic retrieved operative Education provisions below that root, although the publisher's interface does not render it. It must not be discarded or counted as an empty container. Full traversal and independent rendered coverage remain unvalidated for this configuration.
-
-A subsequent metadata-only HEAD request to Cupertino's source-linked Water-Efficient Landscape Checklist PDF returned HTTP 403 on October 17, 2026 at 22:21 UTC. No response body was saved. The shared publisher stop was retained and no further Gridics requests were made by that probe. This is evidence about that exact PDF request; it does not establish that every Gridics route is unavailable. The current workflow conservatively stops live acquisition until authorized access is available. Offline verification and replay remain usable.
-
-The current image normalization also preserves video poster positions as `[IMAGE]`, keeps available captions, and hash-redacts embedded poster payloads. The 26-test family suite, including complete actual publisher-root fixtures and the complete Cupertino ordinance index, passed against this revision. Run it with:
